      "The chemistry behind this depletion is driven by chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), which were once widely used in refrigeration and aerosol sprays.",
      "When CFCs reach the stratosphere, ultraviolet radiation causes them to break down, releasing highly reactive chlorine atoms.",
      "A single chlorine atom acts as a catalyst in a destructive cycle. It reacts with ozone molecules, breaking them down into oxygen, while remaining intact to repeat the process.",
      "This catalytic cycle allows a relatively small amount of CFCs to destroy a disproportionately large number of ozone molecules over time.",
      "The discovery of the 'ozone hole' over Antarctica alerted the international community to the urgency of the problem, leading to the Montreal Protocol in 1987.",
      "The Montreal Protocol is widely regarded as one of the most successful international environmental agreements, mandating the phase-out of ozone-depleting substances.",
      "As a result of global compliance, atmospheric concentrations of CFCs have declined, and scientists have observed signs of recovery in the stratospheric ozone layer.",
      "However, the process is slow. Because these chemicals have long atmospheric lifespans, they continue to impact the stratosphere for decades after their release.",
      "The success of the Montreal Protocol provides a valuable blueprint for international cooperation in addressing other global environmental challenges, such as greenhouse gas emissions."
